## Summary
Fixes #50

The default `onopen` handler now checks `response.ok` before checking content-type. This provides better error messages for HTTP errors (404, 401, 500, etc.) instead of cryptic "Error in body stream" errors.

## Changes

**Before:**
```typescript
function defaultOnOpen(response: Response) {
    const contentType = response.headers.get('content-type');
    if (!contentType?.startsWith(EventStreamContentType)) {
        throw new Error(`Expected content-type to be ${EventStreamContentType}, Actual: ${contentType}`);
    }
}
```

**After:**
```typescript
function defaultOnOpen(response: Response) {
    if (!response.ok) {
        throw new Error(`Request failed with status ${response.status}: ${response.statusText}`);
    }
    const contentType = response.headers.get('content-type');
    if (!contentType?.startsWith(EventStreamContentType)) {
        throw new Error(`Expected content-type to be ${EventStreamContentType}, Actual: ${contentType}`);
    }
}
```

## Behavior
- Error is thrown and triggers `onerror` callback with descriptive message
- Users can inspect `err.message` to get status code (e.g., "Request failed with status 404: Not Found")
- Users can decide whether to retry or stop in their `onerror` handler

## Test plan
- [x] Added test for 404 response
- [x] Added test for 500 response  
- [x] All 39 tests pass
- [x] Build succeeds

